exercises.solution:
```python
def invariant_factors(orders):
    powers = {}                      # prime -> list of prime powers
    for n in orders:
        p = 2
        while n > 1:
            q = 1
            while n % p == 0:
                n //= p
                q *= p
            if q > 1:
                powers.setdefault(p, []).append(q)
            p += 1
    k = max((len(v) for v in powers.values()), default=0)
    factors = [1] * k
    for v in powers.values():
        v.sort(reverse=True)
        for i, q in enumerate(v):
            factors[k - 1 - i] *= q
    return factors
```

The largest invariant factor collects the largest power of each prime, the next one the second largest, and so on; this is exactly how the uniqueness proof of the structure theorem recovers invariant factors from elementary divisors. For $[12, 18, 8]$ the $2$-powers are $8, 4, 2$ and the $3$-powers $9, 3$, giving $d_3 = 72$, $d_2 = 12$, $d_1 = 2$.
